              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "cōnsequor, cōnsequī — \"to follow; to achieve, to obtain\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Quod voluit, cōnsecūtus est. He achieved what he wanted."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Deponent: cōnsequor, cōnsequī, cōnsecūtus sum. Cōnsequitur ut, it follows that. English consequence."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "cōnsequor is built from con- and sequor \"to follow\" (EtymDB; Lewis & Short): to follow all the way to the goal. English consequence comes from its family."
            }
          ]
        },
