          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "redundō, redundāre — \"to overflow; to result (in), to fall back (on)\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Haec culpa in tē redundat. This fault falls back on you."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "redundō, redundāre, redundāvī, redundātum. Built on unda, a wave: water flowing back over, and blame or credit coming back. English redound and redundant."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "redundō is built from red- \"back\" and undō \"to surge\", from unda \"a wave\" (Lewis & Short). English redound and redundant come from it."
            }
          ]
        },
